# “The Shop Has Opened!”
Tamil title: “கடை திறந்தாச்சி!”

“An intelligent man is always intelligent,” declared former arrack contractor Muthusami Pillai with delight. Beside him stood Parthasarathi Iyengar, who had shortened his name to Sarathi, manager of Muthu Talkies, smiling as if in agreement. Muthusami ordinarily neither praised intellectuals nor consented to see them. Amirthalingam was therefore surprised. Owing Muthusami three thousand rupees, he had mortgaged property worth twenty-five thousand and treated it as a duty to visit daily and speak pleasantly.

“Yes, indeed,” said Amirthalingam, his customary daily instalment of “interest.”

“Ask him why. Merely saying yes will not do,” Sarathi prompted.

“He is going to tell us,” Amirthalingam replied politely.

Muthusami burst out: “These fellows leapt about without knowing head from foot. They crowed that anyone criticising prohibition would have his tongue rot, would be ruined, and would not be spared by the Congress government. See what the intelligent man has now given them, blow after stinging blow: ‘Enough of your experiment; recognise the truth!’ Would an intelligent man fear such fellows? He does not need their favour; they may need his!”

Seeing Amirthalingam struggle to understand whom he called intelligent and whom he abused, Sarathi explained. “You do not follow, do you? Our newspaper, *The Hindu*, has published something extraordinary. It has thoroughly rebuked the government: its course is wrong; walking with closed eyes, it cannot distinguish hill from hollow and will meet danger; it must open its eyes.”

Amirthalingam grew more bewildered. “Would *The Hindu* criticise the government? Haven’t you repeatedly said they are one and the same?”

Muthusami supported Sarathi. “*The Hindu* supports the government only while it avoids the wrong path. Must it endure however the government dances and runs? Can all the government’s pens fight the pen of *The Hindu*?” Then, turning deferential, he added, “Swami, it was fortunate you kept that grove in our hands this year too.”

Sarathi replied that he had always advised against wasting even old rope, since everything might again be needed.

Muthusami had not believed him. A minister entertained at their bungalow had proclaimed at an evening meeting that prohibition was succeeding, that police were suppressing occasional violations, and that the policy would never be revoked. Since hearing that categorical declaration, Muthusami had lost hope that the old shops would return.

“Do you mean the toddy shops will reopen?” Amirthalingam asked in astonishment.

Sarathi said that was precisely the point. Prohibition had become riddled with holes and yielded no worthwhile result. It had merely placed new taxes upon the public. Illicit toddy and arrack remained freely available. Why cling to the scheme? Rules might limit how much one drank and prevent drunken disorder; advice might urge moderation. Such measures could achieve something, but prohibition itself produced nothing. *The Hindu* had explained this clearly.

“Say it wrote bravely!” Muthusami exclaimed. “Would other newspapers possess such courage? An intelligent man is an intelligent man. ‘I shall write the truth; what do I care whether you govern a town or the whole world?’ That courage distinguishes *The Hindu*.”

Muthusami knew only enough English to sign his name. Amirthalingam wondered how he had read the paper, but dared not ask his creditor. Instead he delicately asked Sarathi when he had seen it. Muthusami explained that his morning coffee had tasted bitter and left him angry; Sarathi arrived, asked whether he had heard the news, and rapidly read the paper aloud.

Sarathi now took up *The Hindu* and paraphrased it. The expected benefit of prohibition must be tested; one must not insist it was sacred and therefore unalterable. Drinkers had not stopped. They paid more for illicit liquor and other intoxicants. Police arrested many, but the hunt never ended. Respect for the law had not stopped illicit distillation, sale, or drinking; prisons were crowded with offenders. Meanwhile government had lost seventeen crores in revenue, sales tax had risen, and the poor bore the burden. The law produced no benefit and heavy loss. Instead of remaining immovable like a stone Pillaiyar, government could advise people not to drink excessively, ruin their families, or become ceaseless drunkards, and could regulate the number of shops rather than ban them entirely. A committee should examine whether prohibition ought to remain or be repealed.

Muthusami again praised the paper’s courage. Sarathi said Madhya Pradesh had already appointed a committee to consider prohibition and that *The Hindu* explicitly sought one here too.

Amirthalingam asked whether appointing a committee meant repeal. Muthusami answered that mountains of evidence proved prohibition an utter failure. Sarathi added that a committee itself admitted the scheme was defective. If government openly announced that toddy shops would reopen, it would look unbecoming. A committee could investigate, pronounce prohibition useless, and let government repeal it honourably on the committee’s advice.

Amirthalingam agreed. Muthusami scoffed that after *The Hindu* had spoken, prohibition could not long survive.

Amirthalingam nevertheless foresaw public anger. For years Congress had preached that drinking was sinful and ruined people, denounced the British for opening liquor shops, enacted prohibition with ceremonial fervour, and sent ministers from town to town replacing liquor shops with coffee, dry-ginger-water, and sweet-drink stalls. It published pictures and songs, claimed families were joyful, poor people had saved lakhs, violent crime had fallen, and society had gained new vitality. It demanded that prohibition be guarded like one’s eye and that not even the smell of toddy remain if Gandhi’s soul were to find peace. After all this, could it now call the law a paper gourd and repeal it? People would ask why an unworkable law had ruined the country, imposed tax after tax, and led Congress to insult those who had predicted failure.

Sarathi confidently replied that people would speak at first; after *The Hindu* wrote twice more as it had now, the noise would subside. Muthusami rejoiced that once intellectuals declared prohibition useless, no one would heed the public clamour.

Amirthalingam still called legislation followed by repeal wasted effort. Sarathi disagreed. Even without eliminating drink, prohibition had brought Congress fame for two or three years. Whenever critics asked what Congress had achieved, its supporters could silence them by pointing to prohibition as the greatest good. Was that not profit?

Muthusami added another advantage. Whenever people demanded schools, bridges, and other works, ministers blamed the many crores lost through prohibition for their inability to act. The people accepted this because prohibition was a sacred enterprise. Had the law not greatly profited Congress?

Amirthalingam concluded that toddy shops would soon reopen. Sarathi thought otherwise, startling Muthusami. Reopening them before an election would create enormous difficulty for Congress. It would first complete and win the election, then appoint a committee, cite its findings, and repeal prohibition. That seemed the strategy.

Muthusami anxiously asked whether Congress feared electoral defeat if it reopened the shops. Amirthalingam saw Sarathi’s point: voters would accuse Congress of hypocrisy, recalling its claims to have closed the shops, removed sin, and protected the poor, only to reopen them and prove its words and deeds worthless.

Muthusami sprang up angrily. “Let them talk! Will that settle it? Will people like us remain idle? Will we not march shouting ‘Victory to Congress’? With our support, why should Congress fear an election?”

Sarathi’s face brightened. He called himself a fool for missing the obvious: repealing prohibition would bring Congress immense support.

“Call it spectacular support, Swami!” Muthusami corrected him. “We shall lead voters in processions from our own shops and make them vote for Congress.”

Amirthalingam did not dispute it. He took leave and returned home troubled.

His only daughter was Bharathi. That very day a letter had informed him that the Madras government was certain to employ Bharathi’s husband as a prohibition propagandist. How could he not be worried?

At home his younger brother Varadarajan waited at the entrance and smiled: “The shop has opened.”

Amirthalingam snapped, “All right! Have you come merely to tell me that shameful news?” Varadarajan faltered and asked why he was being attacked.

Recognising him, Amirthalingam apologised. He had been preoccupied and distressed. “What did you say?”

“The shop has opened, anna.”

“Which shop?” Amirthalingam asked, again bewildered.

“The khadi shop!”

Amirthalingam remembered that his brother had discussed it for three months. Varadarajan said it had opened that day because Sarathi declared the day auspicious.

(Published in *Dravida Nadu*, 28 January 1951.)

---NOTES---
The title’s “shop” is deliberately ambiguous: the dialogue anticipates reopened toddy shops, while the closing line refers to Varadarajan’s khadi shop.
The speakers use colloquial Tamil and Sarathi’s Brahmin dialect; the translation preserves differences in tone without reproducing dialect as caricatured English.
Scans `005-e7c58cf87e.png` and `006-876b5843f9.png` contain an unrelated essay about Buddhism and Brahminical dominance. They were excluded from this canonical source because scan 4’s prohibition discussion continues directly on scan 7. The physical image files remain preserved for later reassignment.
